✅ Scrum (most relevant)
- Sprints usually 1–4 weeks (commonly 2)
- Ceremonies: Planning, Daily Scrum, Review, Retrospective
- Roles: Product Owner, Scrum Master, Developers
- Fits feature teams (e.g. B-SMS, OBD-style product work)


👥 Scrum Roles
- **Product Owner**: backlog ownership, priority, represents customer/business
- **Scrum Master**: coach/facilitate, remove blockers, protect Scrum process (not a traditional “manager”)
- **Developers**: cross-functional team that builds the Increment (devs, QA, etc.)


📦 Scrum Artifacts (interview-important)
- **Product Backlog** — ordered list of everything that might be needed (owned by PO)
- **Sprint Backlog** — items selected for this sprint + plan to deliver them
- **Increment** — usable product addition at sprint end (meets Definition of Done)

Related terms:
- User Story → small valuable requirement (“As a… I want… so that…”)
- Story Points / Velocity → relative sizing & how much a team usually finishes
- DoD (Definition of Done) → shared quality checklist before “Done”
- Backlog refinement/grooming → clarify & size upcoming items


🧪 Scrum Ceremonies
- **Sprint Planning** — what + how for this sprint
- **Daily Scrum (standup)** — ~15 min; typically: yesterday / today / blockers
- **Sprint Review** — demo Increment, get stakeholder feedback
- **Sprint Retrospective** — team improves process (what went well / improve / actions)


🛠️ Agile in Practice (Java developers)
- Work in sprints; ship small backend slices (APIs, services)
- Daily standups; tasks in Jira/Trello
- Collaborate with PO/QA; refine stories
- CI/CD (Git, Jenkins, Docker, K8s) supports frequent, safe releases


✍️ Sample Interview Answer
"I work in an Agile environment following Scrum. We run 2-week sprints with planning, daily stand-ups, review, and retrospectives.
As a Java developer I deliver backend features, join backlog refinement, and work closely with QA and product.
That helps us ship working increments often and adjust quickly from feedback."


🧠 Interview one-liner
“Agile is iterative delivery guided by the Manifesto; Scrum is a popular framework with roles, sprints, artifacts,
and ceremonies that turn feedback into the next Increment.”
